      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The First Catawba Baptist Church, founded in 1773, has long served as the town’s historic gathering place.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nathalie_virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Clarkton Bridge, listed on the National Register in 2006, links Nathalie to the Roanoke River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nathalie%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A nearby Amish community hosts more than 30 businesses, from furniture shops to sawmills, preserving horse‑drawn buggies and Sunday closures.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nathalie_virginia",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Nathalie lies about four miles southwest of the Roanoke River, its waters fed by tributaries of Catawba Creek.",
